;; Reviewed 2026-10-10 by the user (written by Claude, from the design,
;; the MACRO manual, LLVM's code generator and the debugger runs). Why
;; this is right:
;; - Phase 16 step 3 (section 14, item 8): a nested procedure is lifted
;;   to a procedure of its own, a local label and .WORD of its mask,
;;   never .ENTRY, since only its enclosing procedures call it. Its
;;   symbol is made from its enclosing procedure's full name and its
;;   own, VaxNested.Deep.Middle.Inner, or
;;   VaxNested.CounterDesc.Bump.Once in a type-bound procedure, so
;;   Basic's Add and Sum's Add differ. Each one is written before the
;;   procedure it is nested in, parents first.
;; - After its own parameters it takes one hidden group per variable of
;;   the enclosing procedures it needs (NestedProcedures.Analyze, as on
;;   LLVM), outermost procedure first, then in declaration order: the
;;   variable's address, then a VAR record parameter's tag (Kind.Test,
;;   8(AP)) or an open array's lengths (Sum.Add, 8(AP) and 16(AP)). It
;;   uses each as a VAR parameter, through @n(AP). A procedure that only
;;   passes a variable on still takes it: Deep.Middle takes Deep's a and
;;   b for Inner, and Cousins.B.C takes x to call A.
;; - A call passes, for each variable, what the caller has for it: the
;;   address of its own local (MOVAB -4(FP)), the address it was given
;;   (MOVL 4(AP)), or an open array's copy (MOVL -4(FP)), and the tag or
;;   lengths beside it.
;; - A value parameter or receiver a nested procedure needs is copied to
;;   the frame on entry (Deep's a, Bump's c and k), since the nested one
;;   may assign it and the argument list is read-only. Bump's Once sets
;;   c to NIL in Bump's copy, the trap run's mode 1.
;; - Recursion of the enclosing procedure (Tree) gives each activation
;;   its own frame, whose here its own Visit reaches.
;; - The debugger runs examine what each check computes, which
;;   NestedOut, built by both backends, also prints. Then trap 4 at line
;;   107, column 11, c in INC(c.n, k), and trap 2 at line 87, column 51,
;;   the index, both where LLVM's -trap-location gives them.
;; - NEW calls rtl/vax's GarbageCollectedHeap.Allocate by its symbol,
;;   G^GARBAGECOLLECTEDHEAP_A_A8NV38EI, .EXTERNAL, where it called
;;   POC_NEW (Phase 16 step 4, section 15, proposal 9): the same
;;   argument list, the size and the tag, and the same result in R0.
;; - Its variables hold pointers, so it has a root table, L_ROOTS in
;;   POC_DATA: a link, which POC_ROOTS sets, the count, 1, and the
;;   address of each pointer (cnt), flattened as a type descriptor's
;;   offsets are. The initializer registers it with POC_ROOTS before it
;;   calls its imports' initializers.
;; - The program allocates, so its start calls the collector's
;;   initializer, GARBAGECOLLECTEDHEAP_INIT, and then SetStackBase
;;   (GARBAGECOLLECTEDHEAP_S_EA118OLO) with its own FP, the base the
;;   collector scans the stack up to, before the module's initializer.
;; - The debugger runs link tools/vax-runtime-stub.mar, whose Allocate
;;   takes memory from LIB$GET_VM as POC_NEW did and never collects.
; VaxNested, written by poc (doc/developer/vax-macro32-backend.md)
	.TITLE	VAXNESTED
	.IDENT	/6a7b710f77b7fe19/
	.PSECT	POC_CODE,  PIC,SHR,NOWRT,EXE,LONG
	.PSECT	POC_CONST, PIC,SHR,NOWRT,NOEXE,QUAD
	.PSECT	POC_DATA,  NOPIC,NOSHR,WRT,NOEXE,QUAD

